-<p><strong>Choosing Z.</strong> Z = 0.8 is a judgment input here, used
-to show the mechanics, and the sample results table further down shows
-how the answer changes with Z. In practice Z comes from how much data
-stands behind the observed rates. For example, the limited-fluctuation
-rule gives full credibility (Z = 1) at about 1,082 deaths, and otherwise
-Z = √(deaths / 1,082). That requires real death counts, which this life
-table (scaled to 100,000 births) doesn’t contain. On the log scale the
-three curves look close. At about half of all ages, the 2021 rate is
-within 5% of the pre-pandemic average, but at some adult ages it is up
-to 51% higher.</p>
