+Explanation of Cox Proportional Hazards Model
 </summary>
 A Cox model compares the hazard (the instantaneous risk of death)
 between groups. Life tables have no individual-level covariates, so we
 compare two calendar periods instead: the 1951 and 2021 tables, each
 expanded into weighted deaths the same way as for Kaplan-Meier. The
-coefficient gives the hazard ratio, how many times higher or lower the
-risk of death is at the same age in 2021 than in 1951. An earlier
-version used Age both as the survival time and as a covariate, and
-treated each table row as a person, so its output had no meaning.
+model assumes <span class="math inline">h_{2021}(x) = e^{\beta} \,
+h_{1951}(x)</span> at every age <span class="math inline">x</span>, so
+<span class="math inline">e^{\beta}</span> is the hazard ratio: how many
+times higher or lower the risk of death is at the same age in 2021 than
+in 1951. An earlier version used Age both as the survival time and as a
+covariate, and treated each table row as a person, so its output had no
+meaning.
 </details>
