+<p><strong>Choosing <span class="math inline">Z</span>.</strong> <span
+class="math inline">Z = 0.8</span> is a judgment input here, used to
+show the mechanics, and the sample results table further down shows how
+the answer changes with <span class="math inline">Z</span>. In practice
+Z comes from how much data stands behind the observed rates. For
+example, the limited-fluctuation rule gives full credibility (<span
+class="math inline">Z = 1</span>) at about 1,082 deaths, and otherwise
+<span class="math inline">Z = \sqrt{n / 1082}</span>, where <span
+class="math inline">n</span> is the number of observed deaths. That
+requires real death counts, which this life table (scaled to 100,000
+births) doesn’t contain. On the log scale the three curves look close.
