+<strong>Overview:</strong><br> We model <span
+class="math inline">q_x</span> as a binomial probability (<span
+class="math inline">d_x</span> deaths out of <span
+class="math inline">l_x</span> alive) with Age and Year as predictors,
+and compare three link functions, which are three different shapes for
+how the predictors map to a probability. We use ages 30-100 for the same
+reasons as the Gompertz model above. An earlier version also used mx as
+a predictor; qx is calculated directly from mx, so that just fed the
+answer into the model.<br><br> <strong>1. Logit:</strong> <span
+class="math inline">\log\dfrac{q}{1-q} = \alpha + \beta x + \gamma
+t</span>. This is ordinary logistic regression.<br><br> <strong>2.
+Probit:</strong> <span class="math inline">\Phi^{-1}(q) = \alpha + \beta
+x + \gamma t</span>, where <span class="math inline">\Phi</span> is the
+standard normal distribution function.<br><br> <strong>3. Complementary
+log-log:</strong> <span class="math inline">\log\big(-\log(1-q)\big) =
+\alpha + \beta x + \gamma t</span>. Since <span
+class="math inline">-\log(1-q)</span> is the cumulative hazard over the
+year, this is equivalent to a log-linear (Gompertz-type) model for the
+force of mortality, so it is the natural choice for mortality.<br><br>
+<strong>Model comparison:</strong> lower AIC means a better fit. Because
+<span class="math inline">l_x</span> is scaled to 100,000 births, the
+AIC values only make sense relative to each other, not on their own.
